# {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}

Built on [pete-mcp-core](https://github.com/pete-builds/pete-mcp-core) — structured logging, healthcheck, transport wiring, and settings all come from the shared library.

`pete-mcp-core` is not published to PyPI. `pyproject.toml` depends on it through a pinned commit tarball (`pete-mcp-core @ https://github.com/pete-builds/pete-mcp-core/archive/<sha>.tar.gz`), so `pip install` fetches it from GitHub and needs network access to github.com. Dependabot cannot see through that pin: to take a newer core, edit the SHA in `pyproject.toml` by hand.

## Install

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

## Run

```bash
# stdio (for Claude Desktop)
python -m {{ package_name }}.server

# streamable-http (for remote MCP clients)
MCP_TRANSPORT=streamable-http python -m {{ package_name }}.server
```

Or with Docker:

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

## Configure

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and edit. All variables are optional; sensible defaults live in [pete-mcp-core's settings](https://github.com/pete-builds/pete-mcp-core/blob/main/src/pete_mcp_core/settings.py).

For production HTTP deploys, set `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN` (generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`) and `MCP_AUTH_REQUIRED=true` to fail closed if the token is unset.

## Test

```bash
pytest
```

CI runs on Python {{ python_min_version }}+ across a matrix.

## Release parity

`.github/workflows/parity.yml` compares each change with the last release on GHCR: the tool list, every tool's description and schema, and the read-only calls in `parity/golden.yaml`. Any difference fails unless `parity/expected.yaml` declares it with a reason. Both containers run on the dummy values in `parity/parity.env`, so add your server's variables there with placeholder values. See the [pete-mcp-template README](https://github.com/pete-builds/pete-mcp-template#release-parity-check) for what it does and does not prove.

